# `yo fix <path> --migrate explicit-copy` follows a copy through the locals
# it is stored in, moves included
# (issues/fixed/2026-10-23-explicit-copy-migration-misses-a-write-through-a-local-the-copy-was-moved-into.md).
# `x := entry.no; target := x; target.push(v)` copies the entry's list into
# `x` and writes it through `target`; the same flow through a block tail, an
# arm that ends in a local, an assignment, and a source moved after the copy
# (`snapshot := list; target := list; target.push(v)`). The migration used to
# clone all six (and list one), and the run printed `1 0 1 0`. Now the copies
# that reach a written local are listed, and only `peek`'s (moved into a
# local that is never written) is cloned. The trailing `build run` runs the
# migrated program: `3 2 2 2` is the behavior the rewrite must keep.
# stdout_keep drops the build chatter.
stdout_keep=^(fix:|[0-9]+ [0-9]+ [0-9]+ [0-9]+$)
timeout=600
